; expect 42
; A float literal whose fractional part begins with a zero, which no
; other file in this repository contains outside a comment - and which
; `axiom fmt` therefore rewrote into a different number for as long as
; the formatter has existed.
;
; `fpFloat` normalised the fractional digits with `fpDigits`, the
; helper that drops LEADING zeros. That is right for an integer part,
; where `007` and `7` are the same number. On a fraction it changes
; the value: `0.05` was printed `0.5`, `0.0583` as `0.583`. Exit 0, no
; warning, and `fmt`'s own round-trip check satisfied, because both
; spellings are perfectly good float literals and the re-scan only
; asks whether a float is there.
;
; This file is the gate. `check-fmt.sh` formats a copy of the whole
; repository and re-runs `check-self-host.sh` against it, so a
; recurrence turns this case's 42 into a 1 - which is why the terms
; below are comparisons that a ten-fold error breaks, rather than a
; printed number nothing reads.
;
;   a  0.05   -> 0.5    under the bug: no longer < 0.1
;   b  0.0583 -> 0.583  under the bug: no longer < 0.1
;   c  1.050  -> 1.05   trailing zeros must STILL go
;   d  0.000  -> 0.0    a fraction of only zeros keeps one digit
;   e  007.50 -> 7.5    an integer part's leading zeros must still go
(:: main Int)

(fn (main)
  (let (
    (a 0.05)
    (b 0.0583)
    (c 1.05)
    (d 0.0)
    (e 7.5)
  )
    (if (&& (&& (< a 0.1) (< b 0.1)) (&& (&& (< c 1.1) (== d 0.0)) (&& (> e 7.4) (< e 7.6))))
      42
      1
    )
  )
)
